L664: cite110†​ L665: 
L666: Flow Control
L667: ###
L668: 
L669: cite111†​ L670: 
L671: Conditional Logic: `or`
L672: 
L673: The `or_` function in Flows allows you to listen to multiple methods and trigger the listener method when any of the specified methods emit an output.
L674: 
L675: Code
L676: Output
L677: 
L678:     `from crewai.flow.flow import Flow, listen, or_, start
L679: 
L680:     class OrExampleFlow(Flow):
L681: 
L682:         @start()
L683:         def start_method(self):
L684:             return "Hello from the start method"
L685: 
L686:         @listen(start_method)
L687:         def second_method(self):
L688:             return "Hello from the second method"
L689: 
L690:         @listen(or_(start_method, second_method))
L691:         def logger(self, result):
L692:             print(f"Logger: {result}")
L693: 
L694: 
L695: 
L696:     flow = OrExampleFlow()
L697:     flow.plot("my_flow_plot")
L698:     flow.kickoff()
L699:     `
L700: 
L701:     `Logger: Hello from the start method
L702:     Logger: Hello from the second method
L703:     `
L704: cite112†Image: Flow Visual image†mintcdn.com When you run this Flow, the `logger` method will be triggered by the output of either the `start_method` or the `second_method`. The `or_` function is used to listen to multiple methods and trigger the listener method when any of the specified methods emit an output.
L705: ###
L706: 
L707: cite113†​ L708: 
L709: Conditional Logic: `and`
L710: 
L711: The `and_` function in Flows allows you to listen to multiple methods and trigger the listener method only when all the specified methods emit an output.
